On 10/11/2010 7:07 AM, Phillip Helbig---undress to reply wrote:
> I'm currently testing combinations of controllers/boxes/disks/cables
> etc.  Narrow/wide is clear: for wide operation, everything has to be
> wide, but other combinations will work except for a wide disk in a
> narrow shelf.  ("Disk" here means an SBB disk including caddy.)  The
> narrow boxes (BA350) have a SCSI connector (the small 50-pin one)
> inside.  Straightforward.  The wide boxes (BA356) have various modules
> which plug in.  Question: is there any difference between plugging in a
> module with a 50-pin connector and one with a 68-pin connector if it is
> connected to a 50-pin controller by the appropriate cable?  In other
> words, is the sole purpose of the 50-pin plugin module to allow one to
> use a cable with 50-pin connectors on each end, as opposed to a
> (possibly more difficult to find) cable with different connectors on
> each end?
>
> Another option is an SBB with a SCSI connector on the front.  Do all of
> these provide additional functionality such as allowing single-ended
> disks on a differential bus, SCSI disks on a DSSI bus etc?
>
> There are 4 common types of SCSI connectors on 1990s-vintage DEC/Compaq
> hardware (on-board or on cards): Centronics 50-pin, the smaller 50-pin,
> the somewhat wider 68-pin and the really small 68-pin.  Are any of these
> normally differential as opposed to single-ended?  Are there connectors
> which are used only for single-ended?  Only for differential?
>
Phillip,

I can't give you exact answers, but I can provide you wiht some more info.

There are TWO types of differential SCSI: older differential ("high 
voltage" differential (HVD), for lack of a better phrase) and 
low-voltage differential.  The older differential SCSI drives were 
supported in the Wintel world mostly by the Adaptec 2944, with both 50- 
and 68-pin connectors.  AFAIK, DEC did not use the HVD drives, which 
were often not labeled as such, but whose model identifier would tell 
you that they are HVD.

Newer LVD-capable drives are mostly capable of BOTH LVD and single-ended 
operation.  You need to check the specs to see how they are configured 
as one or the other.  Often they autoconfigure at boot time, based on 
info sent out by the host bus adapter (HBA).

The 68-pin connector carries both one additional addressing bit (up to 
16 devices per bus, including HBA) and the additional 8 bits of data 
compared to 3-bit device address and 8-bit data bus of 50-pin SCSI.

You can mix 50- and 68-pin SCSI devices on the same bus, but it is not 
recommended.  There are also some limitations imposed by running 68-pin 
devices with a 50-pin HBA connection, namely that they are restricted to 
essentially 50-pin operation.  Some 68-pin devices will not operate at 
all with a 50-pin HBA.  As a rule, 50-pin devices operate OK with a 
68-pin HBA connection, subject to proper cabling and bus termination.

AFAIK, do not even consider attaching SCSI devices to a DSSI controller 
or vice versa... Ben Myers



