I assume your BA356 shelf is a blue one. If so, it is an Ultra-SCSI 
shelf which is capable of the Ultra speeds. The brownish grey shelves 
are capable of Fast-SCSI (10MB/s) only.
The personality module you mentioned is a two channel Ultra-SCSI module. 
It means that it has two completely separate buses. When put into a 
Ultra-SCSI shelf, you have every second slot on bus A and the rest on 
bus B. Depending on the switch settings on the personality module the 
slots get their addresses. There are many combinations to choose from 
and the possible ID's can be any value between 0-15.
The personality module terminates both the buses depending on the 
termination switch settings. Therefore you never need an external 
terminator with the personality modules.

The QLA1040 (maybe KZPBA-BA?) is Ultra-Wide capable, but you can still 
configure the settings to match Fast-SCSI as well.

On SCSI buses the speed used is negotiated and will turn out by the 
slowest device. Therefore it is advisable to avoid slow devices on the 
same bus as faster devices. In this case I recommend to put the CD on 
another bus than the BA356 disk shelf. There's also a smaller 
possibility to end up with two same ID's on the same bus, which makes 
the bus unusable. I also suspect that the CD isn't Wide-SCSI compatible.

If everything is correctly configured on the SCSI bus, all devices show 
up in SRM.

Please also remember that the higher the bus speed the shorter the bus 
lengths can be. Only Differential-SCSI allows for long (up to 25m) bus 
lengths.

I cannot point you to any documentation, but I have some of the manuals 
myself.


Kari





