On 10/17/2010 3:05 PM, Phillip Helbig---undress to reply wrote:
<SNIP!>
>
>> On SCSI buses the speed used is negotiated and will turn out by the
>> slowest device.
>
> Are you sure?
>

The SCSI host bus adapter must be able to send commands to every device 
on the SCSI bus, and every device must be able to see all commands, even 
if not intended for a given device.  Setting up the bus to operate at 
the speed of the slowest device is the only way for each device to be 
cognizant of and be able to manage any bus contention.

It's been that way since the original SCSI-1 days.  The various 
incarnations of the SCSI spec were done to provide a very high degree of 
backward compatibility.  Still, there are combinations of SCSI devices 
of different speeds that make no sense at all.  Far better when possible 
to place devices of like speeds on their own dedicated SCSI HBA... Ben Myers


