In article <4cbb2fe5$0$12245$6bda1f65@news.fne.fi>,
=?ISO-8859-1?Q?Kari_Uusim=E4ki?= <uusimaki@exdecWITHOUTTHISfinland.org>
writes: 

> On SCSI buses the speed used is negotiated and will turn out by the 
> slowest device. Therefore it is advisable to avoid slow devices on the 
> same bus as faster devices. 

Unless I am really confused, this is not true.  I typed "will a slow 
scsi device slow down all devices on the bus" (without the quotes) into 
Google and the first link

   http://aplawrence.com/Bofcusm/107.html

gives what seems to me to be a convincing explanation of why this is not 
the case.  

This is also a really good description of the topic, with much more 
detailed information:

   http://www.milosoftware.com/mike/scsi_ide.html

Don't worry about the title; even if you are interested only in SCSI and 
not in IDE at all, this is well worth reading.  It agrees with the first 
reference above and, for the more detailed analysis, works out the 
numbers to show that this is, in most situations, simply not an issue.

Both should be digested thoroughly by anyone interested in SCSI.

The only time something like this (one "bad" device pulls down other 
"good" devices) is when a device which can only do SE will force LVD 
devices to work in SE mode (but even this won't slow things down, just 
reduce the maximum cable length or possibly the number of drives).



