Phillip Helbig---undress to reply wrote:

> I'm looking for a list of common SCSI controllers on 1990s ALPHA
> hardware, such as the NCR810, the KZPSA and so on together with their
> speeds and an indication which (if any) are not SE (i.e. LVD or HVD).

   Define "common".

   Have you looked at SYS$SYSTEM:SYS$CONFIG.DAT (and a Web
search engine)?

> [...] not SE (i.e. LVD or HVD).

   I believe that you need to worry about only HVD.  An LVD
adapter should be perfectly happy falling back to good-old SE,
if that's what you connect to it.

   My XP1000 systems, for example, have an added KZPBA-CA/CX
card (used for external disks and/or tape).  The KZPBA-CB/CY
is HVD, and looks significantly different -- lots of (yellow)
terminator resistor packs.  As I recall, VMS seemed to
tolerate identical-looking plain Qlogic or made-for-SGI cards,
too, but Tru64 wanted only a genuine KZPBA.  I never tried
putting a non-KZPBA card into a PC, and playing with its
settings there (using a Qlogic utility program).  Might help.
Might not.  Over time, I found enough cheap genuine
KZPBA-CA/CX cards to fill my needs (slots).  (One of my
favorite Ebay purchases was a $5 "K2PBR".  Searching for
"Qlogic" paid off that time.)

   Modern VMS supports more modern cards, too.  I got a cheap
LSI Logic SYM21040-33 ("LSI Logic 1010 Ultra160") card, which
almost worked easily, but SYS$CONFIG.DAT included only the
"-66" version, so I had to add the "-33" to
SYS$USER_CONFIG.DAT ("id = 0x00201000", instead of "id =
0x00211000").  However, this means that standard VMS (like,
say, what's on an installation CD-ROM) can't use this card,
and the (old and stable) XP1000 firmware may or may not be
able to boot from something attached to it.  It's been a
while since I did this playing around, but as I recall, the
XP1000 firmware liked this card, but didn't recognize a newer
LSI-21320-IS (Ultra320) card.  Currently, for example, on the
spare/experimental XP1000:

