Phillip Helbig---undress to reply wrote:

> But this is the disk speed, right?

   Not entirely.  For example, the whole list on my main
rx2600 looks like this:

Scsi(Pun0,Lun0) SEAGATE ST373453LC      DX10 (320 MBytes/sec)
Scsi(Pun1,Lun0) SEAGATE ST336607LC      DS09 (320 MBytes/sec)
Scsi(Pun2,Lun0) SEAGATE ST336607LC      DS09 (320 MBytes/sec)

But, if I connect a SCSI DVD-ROM drive to its external SCSI
port, then I get this:

Scsi(Pun0,Lun0) SEAGATE ST373453LC      DX10 (320 MBytes/sec)
Scsi(Pun1,Lun0) SEAGATE ST336607LC      DS09 (320 MBytes/sec)
Scsi(Pun2,Lun0) SEAGATE ST336607LC      DS09 ( 40 MBytes/sec)
Scsi(Pun6,Lun0) TOSHIBA DVD-ROM SD-M17111005 ( 20 MBytes/sec)

So, I interpret these values as the speeds negotiated between
the adapter and the devices.  In this case, I'd say that the
fast-narrow DVD-ROM drive drops the bus back to "fast", so the
best that the last disk drive can do is fast-wide.  (Knowing
nothing about the SCSI cabling inside this box, I assume that
the first two disks are not on the same bus).  Here, it makes
sense to add another SCSI adapter, and to connect any
(slower-than-Ultra320) external devices to it, instead of to
that built-in external SCSI port.

>  It would be nice if the console or VMS would show something
> similar about the adapter.

   If you know the model of the adapter, then you should know
what it can do.  What you need the computer for is to tell you
what's true for the whole mass of devices, cables,
terminators, and whatever else composes the bus.


Michael Unger wrote:

> [...] Using ceramic capacitors instead of electrolytic ones? [...]

   Probably not.

> [...] There
> are a lot of areas where efficiency can be improved, including "power
> factor correction", making the currect drawn by the power supply follow
> the sine wave of the voltage.

   As I understand it, power factor correction makes a power
supply look more like a resistive load to the electric
utility.  I doubt that it increases the efficiency of the
power supply itself.

      http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_factor


