>   As I understand it, power factor correction makes a power
> supply look more like a resistive load to the electric
> utility.  I doubt that it increases the efficiency of the
> power supply itself.
 
>      http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_factor

Low power factor means more current for the same power.
You are supposed to pay for watts, not volt-amps, but more
current means more loss in the wires, mostly on the power
company side.

Also, if you want to run on a UPS, then you have to take
power factor into account.

-- glen


