>
> The only time something like this (one "bad" device pulls down other
> "good" devices) is when a device which can only do SE will force LVD
> devices to work in SE mode (but even this won't slow things down, just
> reduce the maximum cable length or possibly the number of drives).
>

Seems that I wrote my posting misleading. My mistake.

The basic rule is that the adapter (initiator) and the device (target) 
negotiates the speed between themselves and the result will be the 
highest common nominator. There is a similar rule for the width of the 
bus (8/16-bit). Also the LVD/SE, like you mentioned.

Even if the speed and width negotiation would turn out well, the slower 
device will keep the bus occupied a longer time when transferring data 
and that will slow down the performance as well.

Anyway, to make it simple, it is always best to stick to similar devices 
(wide/narrow, Fast/ultra) on the same bus to avoid performance issues.




