>>     Network is flipped to thinwire and correctly terminated,
>> and external scsi has a terminator. I tried removing the scsi
>> header and power from the motherboard, and removing memory,
>> and swapping memory (all eight slots are populated). ?No change.
>> And there's no graphic card installed at all; S3 is up with a
>> vt420 plugged in as monitor.
>>     Any advice, or vax manual reference, how to proceed trying
>> to troubleshoot I'm clueless what to try next. Thanks,
>> --
>> John Forkosh ( mailto: j...@f.com where j=john and f=forkosh )
> 
> John, page B-3 also reports that the FRU is the system module.
> There's no information in the entire manual which might indicate
> that the main board is dead.

Thanks, Hans, ditto Shadoo (see below).
I hope you're right that the main board isn't irreparably dead,
but it's still eluding my attempts to repair it, including all
your and Shadoo's suggestions...

> from your description I understand that the console (a VT420) remains
> black.

Sorry, I didn't explicitly mention that -- yes, completely black,
no srm console messages at all.  If I don't touch the keyboard,
the vt420's startup "ok" screen just stays there.

> To identify the problem, you might want to remove as much components
> as possible: all memory, disconnect disks and tapes and put the S3
> switch in serial mode.

Tried all that, also removing scsi header and peripheral power cable
from the main board, just in case cables were bad.  Also removed and
reseated the front panel led/switch daughterboard (for no particular
reason).  Case was absolutely empty except for the power supply, main
board, and that daughterboard.

> If the result is the same then perhaps the power supply is at fault,
> so swapping power supplies might show a difference.

Yeah, power supplies seem to be a frequent complaint, although this
one looked good to me.  Nevertheless, swapped it with one from a
working vs4000/60 (both use the same H7819-AA part).  No change,
and the 60 still works with the 90's power supply.

> If not then the main board has a fault somewhere I do not know how
> you could narrow that down.

Well, it was working last time I powered it up, and shutdown
completed normally, and then F5 brought me right to srm >>> console,
from which I powered it down.  Looked fine to me.  So what's
likely to happen between that point and the very next time
it's powered up???  (Power spikes very unlikely -- it's plugged
into a surge protector power strip with a separate switch
that's also turned off, and that's plugged into a ups with yet
another surge protector.)

> The phrase "...but no instruction has been run" seems to indicate
> the VAX processor is at fault.

Or maybe what Shadoo suggested below?...

> It is not a badly terminated ethernet nor a dead battery. In those
> cases you get an extended error message on the console;
> Hans

Yeah, I've done all that from time to time, and always managed
to get an srm prompt from which show config usually said something
useful.  But this box looks totally dead.
P.S. By the way, now that you mention it, where is the toy battery
on the main board?  I don't see anything that looks like it.

> From: shadoooo <shadoooo@gmail.com>
> Hello.
> I don't know if there is some on VAX 4000/90,
> but you could try to move all socketed chips,
> specially EPROMS.  Sometime a small amount of
> oxide on IC pins causes bad contacts.

Thanks, Shadoooo.  I looked carefully and only
found one small one, labelled something like
 TL  SS  ENET   ADRS
 HA  SS  FL09159  9232VD
 AN
 ID  DEC84 FX9319 365A1
where that SS/SS is one big S.
Unfortunately, it's seated more than finger tight,
and I don't have a chip puller tool.  I tried
budging it a bit and reseating it, but guessed that
the probability it's the problem is much less than
the probability I'd do serious damage applying more force.

Thanks, again, Hans and Shadoooo,
-- 
John Forkosh  ( mailto:  j@f.com  where j=john and f=forkosh )


