There was a firmware update for the VAXstation 4000-90A IIRC. The
first VAXstation 4000-90A that I got had a problem with its video
card. Dust in the connector and the card, though properly seated, just
wasn't recognized by the hardware and VMS could figure out the
hardware model.
One attempt was to reload/upgrade the firmware, but in order to do so
you must have access to the console. '

The battery pack is just below the main board, in the corner of the
box where S3 lives. Two AA sized batteries, not a CR2032 fitted on the
board itself.

I have no idea why the system suddenly died on you. Switching power
supplies off may produce nasty spikes on the output. However, a
VAXstation is not completely switched off, like many (if not most)
Alphas it also draws current with the power switch in the 'off'
position, possibly to protect the VAC cpu from spikes. Gold diffusion
may have killed it. Gold contacts on the silicon wafer connect it to
the outside world. Above 70 degrees Celsius or so, gold atoms slowly
mix with the silicon layers below. Once gold atoms end up in a
transistor the chip is gone. Speculation though.
It's a pity because the VAXstation 4000-90A is one of the faster VAX
systems and still useful even with Motif.
John, how old was the machine, 12 years or so?
Hans


