John,
well, I certainly wouldn't give up yet if it were my own system. Which
explains why I have so many VAXstations at home,
see http://members.tele2.nl/hvlems/#clst

From the serial number you provided follows the manufacturing date:
week 7 in 1993, so the machine is nearly 18 years old.
The AB means it was built in Albuquerque, NM.
My own three -90A's were all built in 1994 and hopefully they'll live
a little longer than your -90.
I also have a couple of -60's and they're built between 1990 and 1992.

What caused the demise of your system remains a mystery. The gold
diffusion process is most notable in an operating machine,
the VAX cpu may operate at a temperature of around 50 degrees Celsius,
perhaps a little higher. In a VAXstation 4000-x0(A) there's no
dedicated fan for the cpu. Blocking the sides of the box (where the
ventilation holes are) restricts airflow inside and hence increases
inside temperature.
The RZ29 is supported in the 4000-96 model. That disk isn't listed in
the SOG for the 4000-60 the RZ26 is, which is obvious because that SOG
dates from 1995.
But the RZ29 puts out more heat than an RZ26. My own VAXstations
though also have RZ29's inside, some even three of these.

When I get home this evening I'll locate the firmware notes.
The firmware itself is for a -90A so probably not useful on a model
-90, but the procedure may be useful.

The battery pack location was from memory, sorry for the confusion it
caused. I'll check that this evening too.

Did you lift the entire main board from the box by any chance?
If so, did you notice burn marks on the board?
If there is no visible damage then the power supply socket on the main
board may be a good start to check.
The contacts between plug and socket may be bad, or the soldering
between socket and main board itself may be damaged (tiny cracks, a
bad soldering joint that is not visible).

I used to have unswitched power strips too, but changed to switched
ones last week. This reduces the idle current drawn even with the
mains switch in the off position. I feel that computers are best kept
switched on. No condensation of moisture on components, to temperature
gradients that cause mechanical stress. But the cost of electricity is
ever increasing and it is just too expensive to keep these systems
connected to the mains.
A pc draws about 10 W when powered off. Assuming a VAX or Alpha is in
the same league and taking in account that some Alpha's have two power
supplies that means 40 times 10 Watts. Per annum: 400*24*365=3D 1752
kWhr ! That is at least =80440 per year on idle current alone.
Hans


