One other thing to look for:

NetBSD used to have a problem on the 4000/90 that caused the flash to
become corrupted. The fundamental problem is that the flash on the
4000/90 is write-enabled and sits in the address space used for the
console DZ on the 4000/60; probing the system to discover the model
would accidentally write something to the flash.

I recall somebody writing a flash burner to fix 4000/90s that had been
corrupted by NetBSD, but I'm sure not finding it at the moment.

'Course, the system had to be running to update the flash, so that would
be of limited help to you (the NetBSD corruption just caused one of the
self-tests to fail).
-- 
