Up until now, I've been taking a "suck it and see" attitude to testing 
used equipment.  As long as things are not obviously wired up wrongly, 
there shouldn't be (and haven't been) any problems.

But now I might need to be more careful.

Yes, I can find the part number, do some research and see what it is.  
But since this sometimes involves opening things up and often a lot of 
work just to get a look at the part number, I'd like to avoid it if 
possible.

Apparently HVD and SE SCSI don't mix, neither do HVD and LVD.  Not only 
that it doesn't work, but also that stuff can be damaged.

I'm relatively certain that all of the disks I have are SE (most) or LVD 
(a few).  Controllers: I'm not sure.

Below, I'm talking about only desktop/small tower size stuff, 
ALPHAstation 1200 and smaller, say.  No big iron.  In fact, other than 
the ALPHAstation 1200, all the machines I have are essentially PC size, 
whether desktop or deskside.

Were there any factory-installed HVD controllers on 1990s DEC/Compaq 
hardware?

Which combination can lead to damage: HVD controller and non-HVD disk,
or vice versa, or both? 

What were the fastest controllers shipped with 1990s DEC/Compaq hardware
intended for VMS?  What are the fastest controllers which will work with
1990s DEC/Compaq hardware running VMS?  (I mean the highest speed, not
the part numbers, though an exhaustive list of the latter would be nice 
to have as well.)



