In comp.sys.dec Phillip Helbig---undress to reply <helbig@astro.multiclothesvax.de> wrote:
>
> Yes, I can find the part number, do some research and see what it is.  

That is definitly recommended. Mixing HVD and LVD can be very bad
for either parts. In the 90ies, HVD was popular mostly for bigger
external disk packs. There have even been converters, so you can't
always tell by looking at the disks. For example, I remember disk
shelfs with normal narrow SE disks inside, but the shelf had HVD
to connect to the host computer. This may sound confusing, but
back in the days, SE SCSI was narrow, while differential SCSI
was wide, so it did make sense. The problem is that LVD SCSI
uses the same SCSI connector as HVD. They should have chosen
a different connector.

Dennis

