> you have to press the button when it finishes?

Hi,

Firstly, I plugged it into a power meter. Whether this is 100%
accurate or not, I don't know, however, as I have run a few boxes in
my time ;) I can say that the amount of heat it kicks out is
comparable with the figure of 370 watts.

Secondly - the ZX6000 is pretty much an RX2600. The differences are:

1. It only comes with one power supply.
2. It has the Office Friendly option installed, which means less fans
with more control (substantially less noise)
3. Some of the most recent RX2600 specific firmware will not install
(as it identifies itself as a ZX6000 rather than a RX2600)
4. The card cage has an AGP slot - OpenVMS and HP/UX do not support
AGP graphics cards. Install a PCI ATI 7500 instead. Often ebay
purchases are boxes that originally had windows installed so may come
with a stonking but useless (for VMS) AGP graphics card)
5. It requires that a real USB keyboard (i.e. not PS/2 to USB
keyboard) be connected at boot (otherwise you get very strange error
messages). Some people have reported that their particular ZX6000
doesn't exhibit this trait.
6. It might come with the tower sides (which make it look a very
distinctive and impressive box!)
7. The VGA port on the management card doesn't work when installed in
a ZX6000 (you can still get to the management card via serial or
network connections)

It boots quickly and powers down to full power off just the same as
the RX2600. The only difference I've found is that the most recent
firmware for the RX2600 will not install on the ZX6000, and possibly
the most recent management card software (I however would say don't
bother with the management card unless it comes for free - you can
still control the power state of the box with the inbuilt
microcontroller). None of these minor issues have stopped me from
installing OpenVMS, HP/UX and Linux. It runs all very well.

As for the 1GHz processors, I cannot comment - certainly Hoffs info
would point to possible VMS compatibility problems. However, I
personally would be surprised if it didn't work (the official line on
these things is often an ass-covering exercise).

There are some pictures of the ZX6000 in tower configuration here
(apologies for quality they were taken on a camera phone)

http://wickensonline.co.uk/images/Image0030.jpg

http://wickensonline.co.uk/images/Image0031.jpg

http://wickensonline.co.uk/images/Image0032.jpg

Regards, Mark.


