> seems to get close, the system seems always to try to obey the
> auto_action setting (RESTART), and boots.

That was it!!!!!!!

I first connected keyboard and mouse up and powered it up.  I misread
your post and typed Ctrl/X on the PS/2 keyboard.  I then typed Ctrl/X on
the VT320 keyboard and that got me to the >>> prompt.  Console was set
to graphics; I set it to serial.  After a power cycle, it came up with
the serial console.  If the PS/2 keyboard is disconnected, this brings
an error, but other stuff continues.  It obeys the auto_action setting
(RESTART) and tries to boot (but fails since it doesn't find the disk). 

It seems that, as you mention, Ctrl/X is close, but not quite there.  
(After setting the console to serial, it remembers that, even after a 
power cycle.)  When I power it up, there seems to be nothing I can type 
to get me immediate console access.  Ctrl/X gives me >>> but the 
autonegotiation, boot etc continue.  Since neither ethernet nor disk is 
connected at the moment, they both fail, eventually getting me to the 
console.

This is good enough.  Since I use external disks, I can always pull a 
cable to force the boot to fail if I can't figure out how to get to the 
console prompt after a power cycle.  (I normally have RESTART as the 
AUTO_ACTION; presumably HALT would stop at >>>, but that's not the 
setting I want.)

I'll test it out (check the video and external SCSI connectors
(apparently, there are separate controllers for inside and outside the
box) then replce the DEC 3000/600 in my cluster with the XP1000.  That
will be a jump: EV4 to EV6, 175 Mhz to 667 MHz, 192 MB to 2 GB,
certainly a better graphics card.  I have a couple of similar machines 
(though not as well stocked) and a DS10 as backups/replacements.  Once 
the XP1000 is in the cluster, I'll replace the VAXes with Personal 
Workstations (au) (also enough for spares) which will give me something 
more modern.  I really like the VAXen, but I need newer features in 
TCPIP and HBVS and DCL.  I also like the concept of having the cluster 
members more or less identical, at least in terms of software, so that 
it doesn't matter which nodes are in the cluster.  Also, I can maintain 
just ALPHA LPs and third-party stuff, rather than VAX as well.  (I do 
have another use for the VAXen, though, so they will stay in service.)



