Phillip Helbig---undress to reply schrieb:

> There are 4 common types of SCSI connectors on 1990s-vintage DEC/Compaq
> hardware (on-board or on cards): Centronics 50-pin, the smaller 50-pin,
> the somewhat wider 68-pin and the really small 68-pin.  Are any of these 
> normally differential as opposed to single-ended?  Are there connectors 
> which are used only for single-ended?  Only for differential?

With "modern" hardware one can mix LVD/SE (logo on the device)
and 50/68 pin with the appropriate adapters (cables).
There is, however, a fatal difference between high voltage
and low voltage (HVD and LVD) devices, as I learned the hard way.
Mixing these two not only doesn't work, it might also fry
the device and/or the host adapter (I was lucky on that one).
HVD nowadays is mostly legacy, I guess, but the bad thing
is that it's not always indicated on the devices.
The connectors are "normal" fast/wide 68 pins,
but you'd have to look up the product manual to be sure what's inside.
Some disks have the special HVD logo, some don't.
Seagate disks ending with "WD" for example are HVD.



